<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>multilevel data, Kenya child-health programme</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A Kenyan child-health programme is being evaluated using survey data collected from children in several counties. Survey clusters received either <strong>Programme A</strong> or <strong>Programme B</strong>. We want to investigate which programme is associated with a higher proportion of fully vaccinated children.</p>

<p>Download the dataset for your question below. Each row represents one child.</p>

<p><a href="[[textdownload name="kenya_child_health.csv"]]{@stack_csv_formatter(kenya_data,kenya_columns)@}[[/textdownload]]">Download the CSV dataset</a></p>

<p>Use a statistical software package to analyse the data and answer the following questions. Give all percentages to <strong>one decimal place</strong>.</p>

<h5>a. Programme A</h5>

<p>What percentage of children receiving <strong>Programme A</strong> were fully vaccinated?</p>

<p>[[input:ans1]] % [[validation:ans1]]</p>
[[feedback:prt1]]

<h5>b. Programme B</h5>

<p>What percentage of children receiving <strong>Programme B</strong> were fully vaccinated?</p>

<p>[[input:ans2]] % [[validation:ans2]]</p>
[[feedback:prt2]]

<h5>c. Overall comparison</h5>

<p>Based on these overall vaccination percentages, which programme appears to have the higher vaccination rate?</p>

<p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>
[[feedback:prt3]]

<h5>d. Vaccination rates within wealth quintiles</h5>

<p>Now calculate the percentage of fully vaccinated children for each programme within each wealth quintile. Give all percentages to <strong>one decimal place</strong>.</p>

<table class="table">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Wealth quintile</th>
            <th>Programme A (%)</th>
            <th>Programme B (%)</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>1</td>
            <td>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</td>
            <td>[[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>2</td>
            <td>[[input:ans6]] [[validation:ans6]] [[feedback:prt6]]</td>
            <td>[[input:ans7]] [[validation:ans7]] [[feedback:prt7]]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>3</td>
            <td>[[input:ans8]] [[validation:ans8]] [[feedback:prt8]]</td>
            <td>[[input:ans9]] [[validation:ans9]] [[feedback:prt9]]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>4</td>
            <td>[[input:ans10]] [[validation:ans10]] [[feedback:prt10]]</td>
            <td>[[input:ans11]] [[validation:ans11]] [[feedback:prt11]]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>5</td>
            <td>[[input:ans12]] [[validation:ans12]] [[feedback:prt12]]</td>
            <td>[[input:ans13]] [[validation:ans13]] [[feedback:prt13]]</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h5>e. Comparison within wealth quintiles</h5>

<p>Which programme has the higher vaccination rate within <strong>every wealth quintile</strong>?</p>

<p>[[input:ans14]] [[validation:ans14]]</p>
[[feedback:prt14]]]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The calculations in this question can be carried out using a statistical software package. The key quantities are the proportions of fully vaccinated children, first for each programme overall and then separately within each wealth quintile.</p>

<h5>a. Programme A</h5>

<p>The percentage of children receiving <strong>Programme A</strong> who were fully vaccinated is {@float(round(10*ta1)/10)@}%.</p>

<h5>b. Programme B</h5>

<p>The percentage of children receiving <strong>Programme B</strong> who were fully vaccinated is {@float(round(10*ta2)/10)@}%.</p>

<h5>c. Overall comparison</h5>

<p>Based on the overall vaccination percentages, <strong>Programme A</strong> appears to have the higher vaccination rate.</p>

<h5>d. Vaccination rates within wealth quintiles</h5>

<p>When the vaccination percentages are calculated separately within each wealth quintile, the results are:</p>

<table class="table">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Wealth quintile</th>
            <th>Programme A (%)</th>
            <th>Programme B (%)</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>1</td>
            <td>{@float(round(10*ta4)/10)@}</td>
            <td>{@float(round(10*ta5)/10)@}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>2</td>
            <td>{@float(round(10*ta6)/10)@}</td>
            <td>{@float(round(10*ta7)/10)@}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>3</td>
            <td>{@float(round(10*ta8)/10)@}</td>
            <td>{@float(round(10*ta9)/10)@}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>4</td>
            <td>{@float(round(10*ta10)/10)@}</td>
            <td>{@float(round(10*ta11)/10)@}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>5</td>
            <td>{@float(round(10*ta12)/10)@}</td>
            <td>{@float(round(10*ta13)/10)@}</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h5>e. Comparison within wealth quintiles</h5>

<p><strong>Programme B</strong> has the higher vaccination rate within every wealth quintile. This is the opposite of the comparison obtained from the overall vaccination percentages.</p>

<p>The apparent reversal occurs because the two programmes have different distributions of children across the wealth quintiles. Programme A contains a larger proportion of children from the higher wealth quintiles, where vaccination rates are higher, whereas Programme B contains a larger proportion from the lower wealth quintiles. Consequently, comparing the programmes without taking wealth quintile into account gives a different result from comparing them within each wealth quintile.</p>

<p>This is an example of <strong>Simpson's paradox</strong>: an association observed in aggregated data can reverse when the data are examined within relevant groups. It illustrates why the structure of the data and potentially important grouping variables should be considered when interpreting an overall comparison.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
